export default {"T005400":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Jacobson rings near every point</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000908\"><span>Jacobson scheme</span><code>P000908</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005400\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005400</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Jacobson iff every point has an affine neighbourhood with Jacobson coordinate ring.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The Jacobson property of a space is local, and the affine criterion identifies it on spectra.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01P4\">Stacks 01P4 · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01P4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005400\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005400\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005401":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Jacobson property of Spec R minus its closed point</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000908\"><span>Jacobson scheme</span><code>P000908</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005401\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005401</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Suppose R is Noetherian local with closed point m.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The scheme Spec R minus its closed point is Noetherian and Jacobson.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use the Jacobson property for the specified open of a Noetherian spectrum. In the Zariski-pair case I is contained in the Jacobson radical.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IM\">Stacks 02IM · (1)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005401\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005401\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005402":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Jacobson property of the complement of V(J) in Spec R</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000908\"><span>Jacobson scheme</span><code>P000908</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005402\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005402</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Suppose R is Noetherian with Jacobson radical J.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The scheme the complement of V(J) in Spec R is Noetherian and Jacobson.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use the Jacobson property for the specified open of a Noetherian spectrum. In the Zariski-pair case I is contained in the Jacobson radical.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IM\">Stacks 02IM · (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005402\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005402\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005403":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Jacobson property of the complement of V(I) in Spec R</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000908\"><span>Jacobson scheme</span><code>P000908</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005403\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005403</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Suppose (R,I) is a Zariski pair and R is Noetherian.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The scheme the complement of V(I) in Spec R is Noetherian and Jacobson.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use the Jacobson property for the specified open of a Noetherian spectrum. In the Zariski-pair case I is contained in the Jacobson radical.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IM\">Stacks 02IM · (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005403\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005403\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005404":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for normal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005404\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005404</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is normal.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033J\">Stacks 033J · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033J\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005404\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005404\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005405":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for normal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005405\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005405</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings normal.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033J\">Stacks 033J · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033J\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005405\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005405\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005406":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of normal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005406\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005406</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff it has an open cover by normal open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033J\">Stacks 033J · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033J\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005406\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005406\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005407":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of normal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005407\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005407</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is normal, then each open subscheme is normal.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033J\">Stacks 033J · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033J\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005407\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005407\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005408":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Normality on integral affine charts</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005408\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005408</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be an integral scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Normality is equivalent to integral closedness of the coordinate domain on every nonempty affine open.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Normality of a domain is detected on its prime localizations.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033L\">Stacks 033L · equivalence on nonempty affine opens</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033L\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005408\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005408\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005409":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Component decomposition of a normal scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000909\"><span>Finitely many irreducible components on quasi-compact opens</span><code>P000909</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005409\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005409</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Suppose each quasi-compact open has finitely many irreducible components.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff it is a disjoint union of integral normal schemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Normal local domains force distinct components to be disjoint. Local finiteness makes each component open, and Noetherianity gives global finiteness in the finite case.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0357\">Stacks 0357 · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0357\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005409\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005409\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005410":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Component decomposition of a normal scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005410\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005410</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Suppose X is Noetherian.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff it is a finite disjoint union of integral normal schemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Normal local domains force distinct components to be disjoint. Local finiteness makes each component open, and Noetherianity gives global finiteness in the finite case.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033M\">Stacks 033M · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033M\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005410\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005410\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005411":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Component decomposition of a normal scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005411\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005411</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Suppose X is locally Noetherian.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff it is a disjoint union of integral normal schemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Normal local domains force distinct components to be disjoint. Local finiteness makes each component open, and Noetherianity gives global finiteness in the finite case.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033N\">Stacks 033N · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033N\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005411\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005411\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005412":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Connectedness detects integrality under local Noetherianity</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000029\"><span>Connected</span><code>P000029</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005412\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005412</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be nonempty, normal and locally Noetherian.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is integral iff X is connected.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The normal integral components are open and closed. With nonemptiness, connectedness means precisely one component.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033O\">Stacks 033O · first assertion (nonempty convention made explicit)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033O\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005412\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005412\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005413":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Global functions on a normal integral scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005413\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005413</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be normal and integral.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Its global section ring is a normal domain.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Embed the ring in the function field as the intersection of the local rings. Each is an integrally closed domain.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0358\">Stacks 0358 · normality and integrality of the ring of global sections</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0358\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005413\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005413\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005414":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All stalks for Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005414\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005414</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay iff X is locally Noetherian and the local ring is Cohen–Macaulay at every point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>In a locally Noetherian scheme every point specializes to a closed point. Localizing the closed-point condition gives the corresponding condition at any generalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IP\">Stacks 02IP · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IP\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005414\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005414\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005415":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Closed-point test for Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005415\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005415</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay iff X is locally Noetherian and the local ring is Cohen–Macaulay at every closed point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>In a locally Noetherian scheme every point specializes to a closed point. Localizing the closed-point condition gives the corresponding condition at any generalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IP\">Stacks 02IP · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IP\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005415\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005415\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005416":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005416\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005416</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is Noetherian and Cohen–Macaulay.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IQ\">Stacks 02IQ · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005416\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005416\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005417":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005417\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005417</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings Noetherian and Cohen–Macaulay.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IQ\">Stacks 02IQ · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005417\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005417\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005418":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005418\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005418</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay iff it has an open cover by Cohen–Macaulay open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IQ\">Stacks 02IQ · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005418\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005418\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005419":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005419\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005419</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is Cohen–Macaulay, then each open subscheme is Cohen–Macaulay.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IQ\">Stacks 02IQ · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005419\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005419\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005420":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All stalks for regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005420\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005420</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular iff X is locally Noetherian and the local ring is regular at every point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>In a locally Noetherian scheme every point specializes to a closed point. Localizing the closed-point condition gives the corresponding condition at any generalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IT\">Stacks 02IT · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005420\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005420\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005421":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Closed-point test for regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005421\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005421</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular iff X is locally Noetherian and the local ring is regular at every closed point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>In a locally Noetherian scheme every point specializes to a closed point. Localizing the closed-point condition gives the corresponding condition at any generalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IT\">Stacks 02IT · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005421\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005421\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005422":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005422\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005422</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is Noetherian and regular.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IU\">Stacks 02IU · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005422\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005422\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005423":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005423\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005423</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings Noetherian and regular.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IU\">Stacks 02IU · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005423\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005423\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005424":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005424\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005424</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular iff it has an open cover by regular open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IU\">Stacks 02IU · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005424\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005424\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005425":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005425\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005425</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is regular, then each open subscheme is regular.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IU\">Stacks 02IU · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005425\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005425\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005426":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Dimension from the local rings</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000583\"><span>Dimension of a scheme</span><code>G000583</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000584\"><span>Dimension of a scheme at a point</span><code>G000584</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005426\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005426</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The dimension of X is the supremum of the Krull dimensions of its local rings.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Chains of irreducible closed subsets through a chosen specialization correspond on an affine chart to chains of prime ideals in its local ring. Take suprema over all points.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04MU\">Stacks 04MU · (1) = (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-04MU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005426\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005426\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005427":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Dimension from neighbourhood dimensions</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000583\"><span>Dimension of a scheme</span><code>G000583</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000584\"><span>Dimension of a scheme at a point</span><code>G000584</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005427\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005427</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The dimension of X is the supremum of the dimensions of X at its points.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Chains of irreducible closed subsets through a chosen specialization correspond on an affine chart to chains of prime ideals in its local ring. Take suprema over all points.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04MU\">Stacks 04MU · (1) = (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-04MU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005427\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005427\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005428":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Codimension at a generic point</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000583\"><span>Dimension of a scheme</span><code>G000583</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000584\"><span>Dimension of a scheme at a point</span><code>G000584</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005428\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005428</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let Y be an irreducible closed subset of X with generic point ξ.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The codimension of Y in X is the Krull dimension of the local ring at ξ.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Chains of irreducible closed sets containing Y correspond to chains of primes in the local ring at ξ.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IZ\">Stacks 02IZ · codimension equality</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IZ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005428\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005428\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005429":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Generic points detected by dimension zero</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000583\"><span>Dimension of a scheme</span><code>G000583</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005429\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005429</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let x be a point of X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> x is generic in an irreducible component iff its local ring has Krull dimension zero.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Codimension zero means the closure of x is maximal among irreducible closed subsets.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BA9\">Stacks 0BA9 · generic point criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0BA9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005429\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005429\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005430":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Decomposition of locally Noetherian zero-dimensional schemes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000048\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000048</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005430\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005430</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be locally Noetherian of dimension zero.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is a disjoint union of spectra of Artinian local rings.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Affine Noetherian rings of dimension zero are Artinian and decompose into local factors. Each singleton is open.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AAX\">Stacks 0AAX · disjoint union of local Artinian spectra</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0AAX\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005430\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005430\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005431":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Singleton components in dimension zero</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000048\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000048</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005431\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005431</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be zero-dimensional and quasi-separated.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Every connected component is a point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Affine opens are closed. A quasi-compact open is a finite disjoint union of affine clopen pieces.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CKV\">Stacks 0CKV · components conclusion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0CKV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005431\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005431\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005432":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Quasi-compact opens in dimension zero</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000048\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000048</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005432\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005432</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be zero-dimensional and quasi-separated.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Every quasi-compact open of X is affine.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Affine opens are closed. A quasi-compact open is a finite disjoint union of affine clopen pieces.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CKV\">Stacks 0CKV · quasi-compact opens conclusion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0CKV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005432\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005432\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005433":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Isolated points and neighbourhood dimension</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000584\"><span>Dimension of a scheme at a point</span><code>G000584</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005433\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005433</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let x belong to a locally Noetherian scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The dimension of X at x is zero iff x is isolated in X.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>A zero-dimensional Noetherian affine neighbourhood is a finite discrete space. Conversely use the singleton neighbourhood.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H7C\">Stacks 0H7C · equivalence</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0H7C\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005433\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005433\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005434":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open descent of catenarity</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001172\"><span>Catenary scheme</span><code>P001172</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005434\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005434</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is catenary iff X has an open covering by catenary schemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Codimension chains can be studied in any open neighbourhood of the generic point of their smallest member. On affines they are chains of primes.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IX\">Stacks 02IX · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IX\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005434\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005434\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005435":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Catenarity of every affine coordinate ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001172\"><span>Catenary scheme</span><code>P001172</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005435\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005435</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is catenary iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is catenary.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Codimension chains can be studied in any open neighbourhood of the generic point of their smallest member. On affines they are chains of primes.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IX\">Stacks 02IX · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IX\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005435\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005435\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005436":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Catenarity on an affine cover</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001172\"><span>Catenary scheme</span><code>P001172</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005436\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005436</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is catenary iff X has an affine open covering with catenary coordinate rings.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Codimension chains can be studied in any open neighbourhood of the generic point of their smallest member. On affines they are chains of primes.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IX\">Stacks 02IX · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IX\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005436\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005436\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005437":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Catenarity of locally closed subschemes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001172\"><span>Catenary scheme</span><code>P001172</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005437\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005437</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is catenary, its locally closed subschemes are catenary.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Codimension chains can be studied in any open neighbourhood of the generic point of their smallest member. On affines they are chains of primes.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IX\">Stacks 02IX · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IX\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005437\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005437\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005438":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Local dimension functions on a catenary scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001172\"><span>Catenary scheme</span><code>P001172</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005438\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005438</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a locally Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is catenary iff X admits a dimension function Zariski-locally.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use the correspondence between catenarity and local dimension functions on the underlying locally Noetherian sober space.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02IY\">Stacks 02IY · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02IY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005438\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005438\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005439":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Catenarity detected by local rings</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001172\"><span>Catenary scheme</span><code>P001172</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005439\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005439</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is catenary iff every local ring of X is catenary.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Any interval of primes of an affine chart occurs in the local ring at its upper endpoint.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02J0\">Stacks 02J0 · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-02J0\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005439\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005439\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005440":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All Serre R conditions characterize regular</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000911\"><span>Locally Noetherian scheme regular in a fixed codimension range</span><code>P000911</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005440\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005440</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a locally Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular iff it satisfies (Rₖ) for every integer k ≥ 0.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Each Noetherian local ring has finite dimension. At each point take k equal to that dimension.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B3C\">Stacks 0B3C · equivalence for all indices</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0B3C\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005440\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005440\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005441":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All Serre S conditions characterize Cohen–Macaulay</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000910\"><span>Locally Noetherian scheme satisfying a fixed Serre depth condition</span><code>P000910</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005441\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005441</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a locally Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay iff it satisfies (Sₖ) for every integer k ≥ 0.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Each Noetherian local ring has finite dimension. At each point take k equal to that dimension.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0342\">Stacks 0342 · equivalence for all indices</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0342\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005441\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005441\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005442":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Serre criterion for reduced schemes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000013\"><span>Reduced</span><code>P000013</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000911\"><span>Locally Noetherian scheme regular in a fixed codimension range</span><code>P000911</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000910\"><span>Locally Noetherian scheme satisfying a fixed Serre depth condition</span><code>P000910</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005442\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005442</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a locally Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is reduced iff it satisfies both (S₁) and (R₀).</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Apply the corresponding Serre criterion for Noetherian rings on affine opens.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0344\">Stacks 0344 · two-condition equivalence</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0344\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005442\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005442\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005443":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Serre criterion for normal schemes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000911\"><span>Locally Noetherian scheme regular in a fixed codimension range</span><code>P000911</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000910\"><span>Locally Noetherian scheme satisfying a fixed Serre depth condition</span><code>P000910</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005443\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005443</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a locally Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is normal iff it satisfies both (S₂) and (R₁).</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Apply the corresponding Serre criterion for Noetherian rings on affine opens.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0345\">Stacks 0345 · two-condition equivalence</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0345\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005443\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005443\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005444":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Normal schemes of dimension at most 1</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000011\"><span>Regular</span><code>P000011</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000583\"><span>Dimension of a scheme</span><code>G000583</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005444\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005444</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be normal, locally Noetherian and of dimension at most 1.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is regular.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Normality gives (R₁) and (S₂). With the dimension bound the relevant condition holds up to the full dimension at each point.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BX2\">Stacks 0BX2 · bounded-dimension conclusion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0BX2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005444\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005444\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005445":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Normal schemes of dimension at most 2</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000016\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay</span><code>P000016</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000583\"><span>Dimension of a scheme</span><code>G000583</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005445\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005445</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be normal, locally Noetherian and of dimension at most 2.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Cohen–Macaulay.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Normality gives (R₁) and (S₂). With the dimension bound the relevant condition holds up to the full dimension at each point.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B3D\">Stacks 0B3D · bounded-dimension conclusion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0B3D\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005445\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005445\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005446":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Japanese stalks need not give a Japanese scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005446\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005446</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Consider the one-dimensional Noetherian domain cited in 033T, whose local rings are excellent and whose normalization is not finite.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> All its local rings are Japanese, whereas its spectrum is not Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>If this integral affine spectrum were Japanese then its coordinate domain would be Japanese and in particular its normalization would be finite.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033T\">Stacks 033T · counterexample to the local-ring test</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033T\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005446\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005446\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005447":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Alternate Japanese conditions using associated primes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005447\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005447</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> For the locally Noetherian convention attributed to Hoobler, consider every associated prime of each local ring.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Hoobler tests Japanese quotients by those associated primes. A different possible convention would test associated-prime quotients on all affine opens.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The remark distinguishes the two conventions via the cited example of nonfinite normalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033T\">Stacks 033T · terminology and proposed affine criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033T\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005447\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005447\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005448":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005448\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005448</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be integral.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Japanese iff the coordinate ring of every nonempty affine open is Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033V\">Stacks 033V · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033V\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005448\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005448\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005449":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005449\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005449</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be integral.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Japanese iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033V\">Stacks 033V · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033V\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005449\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005449\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005450":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005450\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005450</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be integral.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Japanese iff it has an open cover by Japanese open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033V\">Stacks 033V · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033V\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005450\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005450\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005451":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005451\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005451</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be integral.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is Japanese, then each nonempty open subscheme is Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033V\">Stacks 033V · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033V\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005451\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005451\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005452":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for universally Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001174\"><span>Universally Japanese scheme</span><code>P001174</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005452\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005452</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is universally Japanese iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is universally Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033W\">Stacks 033W · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033W\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005452\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005452\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005453":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for universally Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001174\"><span>Universally Japanese scheme</span><code>P001174</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005453\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005453</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is universally Japanese iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings universally Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033W\">Stacks 033W · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033W\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005453\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005453\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005454":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of universally Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001174\"><span>Universally Japanese scheme</span><code>P001174</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005454\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005454</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is universally Japanese iff it has an open cover by universally Japanese open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033W\">Stacks 033W · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033W\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005454\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005454\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005455":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of universally Japanese</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001174\"><span>Universally Japanese scheme</span><code>P001174</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005455\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005455</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is universally Japanese, then each open subscheme is universally Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033W\">Stacks 033W · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033W\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005455\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005455\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005456":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for Nagata</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000912\"><span>Nagata scheme</span><code>P000912</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005456\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005456</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Nagata iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is Nagata.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033X\">Stacks 033X · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033X\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005456\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005456\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005457":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for Nagata</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000912\"><span>Nagata scheme</span><code>P000912</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005457\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005457</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Nagata iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings Nagata.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033X\">Stacks 033X · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033X\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005457\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005457\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005458":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of Nagata</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000912\"><span>Nagata scheme</span><code>P000912</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005458\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005458</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Nagata iff it has an open cover by Nagata open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033X\">Stacks 033X · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033X\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005458\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005458\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005459":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of Nagata</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000912\"><span>Nagata scheme</span><code>P000912</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005459\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005459</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is Nagata, then each open subscheme is Nagata.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033X\">Stacks 033X · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033X\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005459\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005459\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005460":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Testing the Nagata property on integral closed subschemes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000912\"><span>Nagata scheme</span><code>P000912</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005460\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005460</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a locally Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is Nagata iff each integral closed subscheme of X is Japanese.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use the criterion that all prime quotients of a Noetherian affine coordinate ring be Japanese.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033Y\">Stacks 033Y · integral closed subscheme criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033Y\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005460\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005460\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005461":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All stalks for a G-scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001175\"><span>G-scheme</span><code>P001175</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005461\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005461</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is a G-scheme iff X is locally Noetherian and the map from the local ring to its maximal-ideal completion is regular at every point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>In a locally Noetherian scheme every point specializes to a closed point. Localizing the closed-point condition gives the corresponding condition at any generalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0HA4\">Stacks 0HA4 · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0HA4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005461\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005461\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005462":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Closed-point test for a G-scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001175\"><span>G-scheme</span><code>P001175</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000009\"><span>Locally Noetherian</span><code>P000009</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005462\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005462</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is a G-scheme iff X is locally Noetherian and the map from the local ring to its maximal-ideal completion is regular at every closed point.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>In a locally Noetherian scheme every point specializes to a closed point. Localizing the closed-point condition gives the corresponding condition at any generalization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0HA4\">Stacks 0HA4 · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0HA4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005462\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005462\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005463":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>All affine charts for a G-scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001175\"><span>G-scheme</span><code>P001175</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005463\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005463</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is a G-scheme iff the coordinate ring of every affine open is a G-ring.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0HA5\">Stacks 0HA5 · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0HA5\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005463\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005463\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005464":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>An affine covering for a G-scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001175\"><span>G-scheme</span><code>P001175</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005464\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005464</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is a G-scheme iff it has an affine open cover with all coordinate rings a G-ring.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0HA5\">Stacks 0HA5 · (1) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0HA5\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005464\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005464\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005465":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Zariski descent of a G-scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001175\"><span>G-scheme</span><code>P001175</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005465\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005465</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is a G-scheme iff it has an open cover by a G-scheme open subschemes.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0HA5\">Stacks 0HA5 · (1) ⇔ (4)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0HA5\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005465\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005465\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005466":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Open restriction of a G-scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001175\"><span>G-scheme</span><code>P001175</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005466\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005466</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is a G-scheme, then each open subscheme is a G-scheme.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The affine criterion follows by refinement with principal opens and descent of the stated ring property on finite principal covers.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0HA5\">Stacks 0HA5 · final assertion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0HA5\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005466\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005466\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005467":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Branch sum over irreducible components</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000587\"><span>Number of branches</span><code>G000587</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005467\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005467</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with a specified point</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Fix x in X. Give each irreducible component through x its reduced induced structure.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The branch count at x is the sum of the corresponding counts for these components.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>For finitely many minimal primes, normalize componentwise in the product of their fraction fields. Infinitely many components force an infinite count.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E20\">Stacks 0E20 · ordinary sum</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0E20\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005467\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005467\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005468":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>A single branch</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001176\"><span>unibranch at a point</span><code>P001176</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000587\"><span>Number of branches</span><code>G000587</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000126\"><span>Unibranch local ring</span><code>P000126</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005468\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005468</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with a specified point</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Fix a point x of a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The branch count is one iff X is unibranch at x.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Apply the local-ring criterion to the henselization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C39\">Stacks 0C39 · (1)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0C39\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/articles/PMIHES_1965__24__5_0/\">Grothendieck–Dieudonné, EGA IV₂, Definition 6.15.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005468\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005468\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005469":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Branch sum over irreducible components (geometric)</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000588\"><span>Number of geometric branches</span><code>G000588</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005469\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005469</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with a specified point</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Fix x in X. Give each irreducible component through x its reduced induced structure.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The geometric branch count at x is the sum of the corresponding counts for these components.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>For finitely many minimal primes, normalize componentwise in the product of their fraction fields. Infinitely many components force an infinite count.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E20\">Stacks 0E20 · geometric sum</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0E20\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005469\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005469\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005470":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>A single geometric branch</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001178\"><span>geometrically unibranch at a point</span><code>P001178</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#G000588\"><span>Number of geometric branches</span><code>G000588</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000127\"><span>Geometrically unibranch local ring</span><code>P000127</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005470\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005470</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with a specified point</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Fix a point x of a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The geometric branch count is one iff X is geometrically unibranch at x.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Apply the local-ring criterion to the strict henselization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C39\">Stacks 0C39 · (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0C39\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/articles/PMIHES_1965__24__5_0/\">Grothendieck–Dieudonné, EGA IV₂, Definition 6.15.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005470\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005470\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005471":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Geometric unibranchness and punctured strict henselizations</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001179\"><span>geometrically unibranch scheme</span><code>P001179</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005471\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005471</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a Noetherian scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is geometrically unibranch iff, at every point other than a generic point of an irreducible component, the punctured spectrum of the strict henselization is connected.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Induct on local dimension. Distinct minimal primes would separate the punctured spectrum once its nonclosed stalks have unique minimal primes.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BQ4\">Stacks 0BQ4 · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0BQ4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005471\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005471\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005472":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Finite generation on an affine scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000142\"><span>Finite module</span><code>P000142</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005472\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005472</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X = Spec A, let M be an A-module and let F be its associated quasi-coherent sheaf.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is of finite type iff M is of finite type.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use principal affine trivializations and localization of M at a prime to compute its stalks.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PB\">Stacks 01PB · Finite generation criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PB\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005472\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005472\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005473":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Finite presentation on an affine scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000143\"><span>Finitely presented module</span><code>P000143</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005473\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005473</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X = Spec A, let M be an A-module and let F be its associated quasi-coherent sheaf.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is of finite presentation iff M is of finite presentation.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use principal affine trivializations and localization of M at a prime to compute its stalks.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PC\">Stacks 01PC · Finite presentation criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005473\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005473\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005474":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Flatness on an affine scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000153\"><span>Flat module</span><code>P000153</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005474\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005474</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X = Spec A, let M be an A-module and let F be its associated quasi-coherent sheaf.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is flat iff M is flat.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use principal affine trivializations and localization of M at a prime to compute its stalks.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P0\">Stacks 05P0 · Flatness criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05P0\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005474\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005474\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005475":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Local freeness on an affine scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000146\"><span>Locally free module</span><code>P000146</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005475\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005475</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X = Spec A, let M be an A-module and let F be its associated quasi-coherent sheaf.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is locally free iff M is locally free.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use principal affine trivializations and localization of M at a prime to compute its stalks.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JM\">Stacks 05JM · Local freeness criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05JM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005475\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005475\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005476":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Finite local freeness on an affine scheme</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000147\"><span>Finite locally free module</span><code>P000147</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005476\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005476</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X = Spec A, let M be an A-module and let F be its associated quasi-coherent sheaf.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is finite locally free iff M is finite locally free.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Use principal affine trivializations and localization of M at a prime to compute its stalks.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JM\">Stacks 05JM · Finite local freeness criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05JM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005476\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005476\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005477":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Functions on a principal open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000046\"><span>Qcqs scheme</span><code>P000046</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005477\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005477</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be qcqs, let f be a global function and let X_f be its invertibility locus.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The global function ring localized at f maps isomorphically to the ring of functions on X_f.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Push forward to the affine spectrum of global functions. The pushforward is quasi-coherent and sections of a quasi-coherent sheaf on a principal affine open are obtained by localization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01P7\">Stacks 01P7 · structure sheaf</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01P7\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005477\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005477\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005478":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Quasi-coherent sections on a principal open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000046\"><span>Qcqs scheme</span><code>P000046</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005478\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005478</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be qcqs, let f be a global function and let X_f be its invertibility locus.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> For every quasi-coherent F, localization of its module of global sections at f maps isomorphically to its sections over X_f.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Push forward to the affine spectrum of global functions. The pushforward is quasi-coherent and sections of a quasi-coherent sheaf on a principal affine open are obtained by localization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01P7\">Stacks 01P7 · arbitrary quasi-coherent module</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01P7\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005478\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005478\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005479":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Injectivity after inverting a section</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005479\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005479</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let L be invertible on X, s a global section and X_s its nonvanishing open. Let F be quasi-coherent and localize its graded module of twisted global sections at s, taking the degree-zero part.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is quasi-compact, the restriction map from the degree-zero localization to Γ(X_s,F) is injective.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Trivialize L on affine charts. A finite cover gives a uniform exponent for annihilation. Quasi-separatedness permits a finite gluing argument on the overlaps.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PW\">Stacks 01PW · (1)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PW\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://wstein.org/sga/ega1.pdf\">Grothendieck–Dieudonné, EGA I, second edition, §6.8, localization by a section</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005479\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005479\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005480":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Recovering sections by inverting an invertible-sheaf section</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005480\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005480</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let L be invertible on X, s a global section and X_s its nonvanishing open. Let F be quasi-coherent and localize its graded module of twisted global sections at s, taking the degree-zero part.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is qcqs, the same map is an isomorphism.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Trivialize L on affine charts. A finite cover gives a uniform exponent for annihilation. Quasi-separatedness permits a finite gluing argument on the overlaps.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PW\">Stacks 01PW · (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PW\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://wstein.org/sga/ega1.pdf\">Grothendieck–Dieudonné, EGA I, second edition, §6.8, localization by a section</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005480\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005480\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005481":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Degree-zero localization of the section ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005481\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005481</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let L be invertible on X, s a global section and X_s its nonvanishing open. Let F be quasi-coherent and localize its graded module of twisted global sections at s, taking the degree-zero part.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is qcqs, degree-zero localization of the section ring at s is the ring of functions on X_s.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Trivialize L on affine charts. A finite cover gives a uniform exponent for annihilation. Quasi-separatedness permits a finite gluing argument on the overlaps.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PW\">Stacks 01PW · structure-sheaf specialization</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PW\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://wstein.org/sga/ega1.pdf\">Grothendieck–Dieudonné, EGA I, second edition, §6.8, localization by a section</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005481\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005481\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005482":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Injectivity for maps on a section open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000143\"><span>Finitely presented module</span><code>P000143</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005482\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005482</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let L be invertible on X, s a global section and X_s its nonvanishing open. Let F,G be quasi-coherent. Form the degree-zero localization of the graded module of maps from F to G twisted by all powers of L.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is quasi-compact and F is of finite type, restriction to Hom on X_s is injective.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On an affine trivialization, finite generation gives a uniform annihilator exponent and finite presentation makes Hom commute with localization. Use a finite cover and finite overlap refinements where required.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01XQ\">Stacks 01XQ · (1)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01XQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005482\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005482\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005483":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Lifting maps on a section open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000143\"><span>Finitely presented module</span><code>P000143</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005483\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005483</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let L be invertible on X, s a global section and X_s its nonvanishing open. Let F,G be quasi-coherent. Form the degree-zero localization of the graded module of maps from F to G twisted by all powers of L.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> If X is qcqs and F finitely presented, the map to Hom on X_s is bijective.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On an affine trivialization, finite generation gives a uniform annihilator exponent and finite presentation makes Hom commute with localization. Use a finite cover and finite overlap refinements where required.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01XQ\">Stacks 01XQ · (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01XQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005483\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005483\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005484":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Sheaves from global sections on a quasi-affine open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000032\"><span>Quasi-affine</span><code>P000032</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005484\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005484</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let U be quasi-compact open in Spec A, and F quasi-coherent on U.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The A-module Γ(U,F) gives an associated sheaf whose restriction to U maps isomorphically to F.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The open immersion is quasi-compact and separated, so the direct image is quasi-coherent.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EHM\">Stacks 0EHM · canonical restriction isomorphism</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0EHM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005484\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005484\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005485":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Affine principal opens of the affinization</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000046\"><span>Qcqs scheme</span><code>P000046</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005485\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005485</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be qcqs, f a global function and X_f affine.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The map to the spectrum of global functions identifies X_f with D(f).</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Both opens are affine and have coordinate ring Γ(X,O_X) localized at f.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01P8\">Stacks 01P8 · canonical identification</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01P8\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005485\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005485\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005486":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Quasi-affineness via affinization</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000032\"><span>Quasi-affine</span><code>P000032</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005486\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005486</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be a scheme.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> X is quasi-affine iff the canonical morphism to the spectrum of its global functions is a quasi-compact open immersion.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On principal affine opens of a quasi-affine presentation this map is an isomorphism onto a standard open.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01P9\">Stacks 01P9 · equivalence</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01P9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005486\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005486\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005487":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Cartesian square of quasi-affine affinization maps</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000032\"><span>Quasi-affine</span><code>P000032</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005487\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005487</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let U → V be an open immersion of quasi-affine schemes.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The inverse image of V under Spec Γ(U,O_U) → Spec Γ(V,O_V) is precisely U, with the canonical maps to both factors.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Check on principal opens contained in V using the localization formula for global functions.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ARY\">Stacks 0ARY · cartesian square</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0ARY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005487\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005487\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005488":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Affine presentation with trivial affine-space pullbacks</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000032\"><span>Quasi-affine</span><code>P000032</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005488\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005488</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let X be quasi-affine.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> There exist n ≥ 0 and an affine scheme T over X such that for each affine X′ over X, its pullback is isomorphic over X′ to affine n-space.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Choose finitely many principal opens covering X in an affine scheme. The unimodular-row construction of the proof becomes affine space after the specified affine pullback.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F82\">Stacks 0F82 · existence with universal affine test</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0F82\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005488\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005488\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005489":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Flat and finitely presented</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001180\"><span>Finite locally free quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001180</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000147\"><span>Finite locally free module</span><code>P000147</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005489\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005489</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Finite local freeness is equivalent to flatness together with finite presentation.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On affine opens apply the finite projective module criteria and their rank function criterion.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P2\">Stacks 05P2 · (4) ⇔ (1)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05P2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005489\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005489\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005490":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Finite presentation and free stalks</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001180\"><span>Finite locally free quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001180</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000147\"><span>Finite locally free module</span><code>P000147</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005490\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005490</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Finite local freeness is equivalent to finite presentation together with freeness of every stalk.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On affine opens apply the finite projective module criteria and their rank function criterion.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P2\">Stacks 05P2 · (4) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05P2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005490\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005490\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005491":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Locally free of finite type</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001180\"><span>Finite locally free quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001180</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000147\"><span>Finite locally free module</span><code>P000147</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005491\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005491</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Finite local freeness is equivalent to local freeness together with finite type.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On affine opens apply the finite projective module criteria and their rank function criterion.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P2\">Stacks 05P2 · (4) ⇔ (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05P2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005491\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005491\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005492":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Finite generation with constant local rank</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001180\"><span>Finite locally free quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001180</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000147\"><span>Finite locally free module</span><code>P000147</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005492\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005492</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Finite local freeness is equivalent to finite type, freeness of every stalk, and local constancy of fibre dimension.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On affine opens apply the finite projective module criteria and their rank function criterion.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P2\">Stacks 05P2 · (4) ⇔ (5)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05P2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005492\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005492\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005493":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Rank test over a reduced base</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001180\"><span>Finite locally free quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001180</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000013\"><span>Reduced</span><code>P000013</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005493\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005493</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X. Assume X reduced.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is finite locally free iff it has finite type and its fibre dimension is locally constant.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>A presentation of the correct fibre rank is locally surjective. Over a reduced ring a relation vanishing at all primes vanishes.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FWH\">Stacks 0FWH · (4) ⇔ (6)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-0FWH\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005493\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005493\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005494":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Testing projectivity on an affine cover</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001181\"><span>Locally projective quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001181</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000149\"><span>Projective module</span><code>P000149</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005494\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005494</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> F is locally projective iff its sections over each member of some affine open cover are projective over its coordinate ring.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Projectivity descends along a finite principal cover and is stable under localization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JQ\">Stacks 05JQ · (1) ⇔ (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05JQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005494\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005494\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005495":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Locally projective sheaves on an affine</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001181\"><span>Locally projective quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001181</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000149\"><span>Projective module</span><code>P000149</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005495\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005495</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let F be quasi-coherent on a scheme X.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> When X is affine, F is locally projective iff its module of global sections is projective.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>Projectivity descends along a finite principal cover and is stable under localization.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JQ\">Stacks 05JQ · in particular</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-05JQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005495\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005495\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005496":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Pullback of locally projective modules</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001181\"><span>Locally projective quasi-coherent module</span><code>P001181</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005496\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005496</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let f be any scheme morphism X → Y and G a locally projective quasi-coherent module on Y.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> The pullback of G is locally projective on X.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>On affines, base change preserves a direct summand of a free module.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/060M\">Stacks 060M · preservation under pullback</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-060M\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005496\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005496\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005497":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Extending a quasi-coherent sheaf</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005497\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005497</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let j:U → X be a quasi-compact open immersion of schemes.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> Each quasi-coherent module on U extends to one on X.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The direct image along j preserves quasi-coherence. Pullbacks of modules along the unit F → j_*j^*F recover prescribed submodules and maps.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PE\">Stacks 01PE · (1)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PE\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005497\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005497\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005498":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Extending a specified submodule</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005498\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005498</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let j:U → X be a quasi-compact open immersion of schemes.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> For F quasi-coherent on X, each quasi-coherent submodule of F|U extends to a quasi-coherent submodule of F.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The direct image along j preserves quasi-coherence. Pullbacks of modules along the unit F → j_*j^*F recover prescribed submodules and maps.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PE\">Stacks 01PE · (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PE\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005498\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005498\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T005499":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"comparison\" data-relation-style=\"criterion\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Scoped comparison</span><h3>Extending a map into a given sheaf</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent algebra-related\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Objects of comparison</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⋯</span><a href=\"../database/#T005499\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T005499</a></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes with specified modules</p><p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> Let j:U → X be a quasi-compact open immersion of schemes.</p><p><strong>Statement.</strong> For F quasi-coherent on X, any map from a quasi-coherent G on U to F|U extends to a map from a quasi-coherent extension of G into F.</p><details><summary>Proof and context</summary><p>The direct image along j preserves quasi-coherence. Pullbacks of modules along the unit F → j_*j^*F recover prescribed submodules and maps.</p></details><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PE\">Stacks 01PE · (3)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PE\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T005499\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#T005499\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>"};
